<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glendalough</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glendalough is a glacial valley in the Wicklow Mountains of eastern Ireland, the site of an early medieval monastery founded by St Kevin in the 6th century. It became one of Ireland's leading monastic centres and places of pilgrimage, and its round tower, cathedral, churches and crosses survive. The monastery was raided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> several times, and in 1214 its diocese was merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glendalough.jpg" alt="Glendalough" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Denzillacey · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Kevin%27s_Kitchen,_Glendalough,_Co._Wicklow_(2023).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Glendalough, from the Irish Gleann Dá Loch, meaning valley of two lakes, lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wicklow">County Wicklow</a>. The valley was formed during the last ice age by a glacier that left a moraine across its mouth. The Poulanass River, which plunges into the valley from the south, built a delta that eventually divided the original lake in two. The massif of Conavalla, 734 metres high, dominates the head of the valley. Semi-natural oak woodland surrounds the lakes, much of it formerly coppiced for wood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> and bark.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kevin, a descendant of one of the ruling families of Leinster, studied as a boy under three holy men, Eoghan, Lochan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a>, and went to Glendalough during this time. He later returned with a small group of monks to found a monastery where two rivers meet. His fame as a holy man attracted numerous followers. He died in about 618, traditionally on 3 June. For the next six centuries the settlement flourished, and the Irish Annals record the deaths of abbots, among them Minndenach in 800, and raids on the site.</p><p>Around 1042 oak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from Glendalough was used to build a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> longship about 30 metres long. At the Synod of Rath Breasail in 1111 the place was designated one of the two dioceses of North Leinster, and the Book of Glendalough was written there about 1131. Laurence O'Toole, born in 1128, became abbot and later Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. The dioceses of Glendalough and Dublin were united in 1214, after which the cultural and ecclesiastical standing of the place diminished. English forces destroyed the settlement in 1398, leaving a ruin that remained a church of local importance and a place of pilgrimage.</p><p>At its height the monastery included workshops, areas for writing and copying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, guest houses, an infirmary, farm buildings and dwellings for monks and a large lay population. The buildings that survive probably date from between the 10th and 12th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The round tower, built of mica-slate interspersed with granite, stands about 30 metres high, with its entrance 3.5 metres above the ground; its conical roof was rebuilt in 1876 from the original stones. Beside it stand the cathedral of St Peter and St Paul, the largest of the buildings, and the stone-roofed St Kevin's Church, known as the Kitchen. The head of the valley was also the site of a galena lead mine from 1825 to 1957, and the valley is now a recreational area with marked walking trails.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Glendalough” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings ruled through tribute and alliances with subject peoples, and great monasteries such as Kildare, <b>Glendalough</b> and Ferns flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-leinster">Kingdom of Leinster</a></li></ul></section>
